// Reactivity S3 — an `effect` observing a `computed` is how the value of a derivation becomes visible
// (and how the differential oracle witnesses recompute order). The effect reads `squared`; on creation
// it runs once (computing `squared` from `n`). Each `n.set(4)` dirties `squared` and reruns the effect,
// which pulls `squared` fresh — so the body echoes "sq" exactly once per rerun. The two identical
// `set(4)`s both rerun (the core does no value-equality suppression), proving a change is a `set`, not
// a value difference.
// expect: stdout "sq"
// expect: stdout "watch=1"
// expect: stdout "sq"
// expect: stdout "watch=16"
// expect: stdout "sq"
// expect: stdout "watch=16"
// expect: exit 0
use std.reactive.{signal, computed, effect}
n = signal(1)
squared = computed(fn() { echo "sq"; return n.get() * n.get() })
effect(fn() {
    echo "watch=${squared.get()}"
})
n.set(4)
n.set(4)
